## 5. The range

### 5a. Execution variance — how long the work takes, given the scope as defined

**Correlation assumption: <independent / partially correlated ρ≈0.5 / fully correlated>** —
<why. Textbook PERT sums variances in quadrature, which assumes independence; on a decomposed
consulting scope the items share the same assumptions and the same team, so when one is wrong
several are wrong together. If σ comes out implausibly tight, this assumption is the bug.>

| | Days |
|---|---|
| ΣO (everything goes right) | |
| ΣM (most likely) | |
| ΣP (everything goes wrong) | |
| **P50** — the planning number | **<n>** |
| **P80** — the commitment number | **<n>** |
| P80 − P50 → contingency (§7) | <n> d |

### 5b. Scope variance — whether this is the work at all

<!--table:scope-->
| Assumption | If wrong | Days |
|---|---|---|
| A<n> | | +<n> |
| | **Upside exposure** | **+<n> d** |

**The two bands are different quantities and are managed with different instruments:** execution
variance → contingency days, carried in the price. Scope variance → assumptions and exclusions,
carried in the contract's words. <State which is larger here, and by how much — on a bounded
scope the scope band is routinely several times the execution band, and that is the single most
decision-useful thing this estimate produces.>

